From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(resources): select native libraries by world version

Servers reference built-in Bedrock textures without redistributing them.
Supply images from the versioned resource bundle shipped with the add-on,
including offline joins. Preserve native layer order, path casing, and
server-pack precedence. Cache native projections for consumers that treat
the shared buffers as immutable. Materialized actor storage retains its
defensive copies.

The 1.26.51.1 game package supplies the missing Hive bread, bucket, and
potion textures. Asset preparation reads local resources without Store
sign-in or package acquisition, removing that dependency from joining.
Report a missing or corrupt bundle as an installation failure.

The matching licensed 1.26.51.1 bundle also supplies native equipment,
models, animations, controllers and material inheritance. Publish these
actor dependencies in the same lower-priority native layers. Preserve
server overrides and exclude native entity declarations that the core
already supplies. Include exact native item and terrain atlas paths for
versioned item icon resolution. Verify the real bundled material families
and equipment definitions without an account.

Cache the projected native pack list and actor layers instead of copying about 50 MiB of texture bytes for every actor and equipment request. Share one accepted-resource selection and inherited model library per resource generation. Cache parsed native animation documents without retaining different server documents in the native cache. Preserve server order, provenance and defensive copies in materialized actor storage. Include the target native item and terrain atlases beneath accepted overrides.

Reuse the bundled sound-caption translation tables in derived actor
libraries. Parse only higher-pack language files instead of every bundled
locale for every native actor type. The October 10 memory-exhaustion log
fails in SoundTranslations during world actor preparation. A regression
checks that 32 actor libraries share the same caption table while server
locale overrides preserve selected-language precedence and fallback.
This removes a confirmed duplication path without claiming the log alone
identifies every retained heap object.

Select immutable vanilla library views using the core world cutoff before
applying accepted server overrides. Keep the raw licensed snapshot shared
for out-of-world account assets and bound cached version views to four.
Older views retain their complete vanilla source so returning to a newer
world restores newer layers instead of filtering an already partial view.

Read archive format2 context through the shared accepted-resource cache.
Carry it into native actor storage, geometry, animation graphs, sounds,
particles, and captions on direct and ViaProxy paths. Include the archive
identity in effect cache retirement. Without accepted world context, use
the existing Java fallback instead of silently selecting latest resources.

Matching BDS1.26.60.6/protocol2225 captures establish wildcard and explicit
1.20.0 base-game inputs. The core patch notes record the executable hash
and Microsoft's cumulative overlay-selection evidence. Regressions cover
newer/older/newer texture, sound and graph selection, retained server
priority, immutable earlier views, eviction/reselection, missing context,
and resource-manager reuse across world-version changes. Native graphical
and audible world-transition comparisons remain pending.

Start one bounded bundled-library preparation during protocol initialization.
Joining awaits the same future, while client startup continues. Publish only
a fully validated library, preserve the original preparation failure across
joins, and stop the worker with the client. This shifts process initialization
out of the first join when startup has enough time; it does not remove its CPU
cost or bypass manifest, resource, version or persona validation.

Regressions cover a join waiting for in-flight startup preparation, repeated
requests reusing one load, and startup failures reaching joining without retry.

Reuse the decoded archive for the same source pack instance, so UI image
lookups in a namespace resource manager share the actor loaders' archive
instead of verifying the nested packs again.

Reuse decoded accepted native sources across resource-manager generations
by ordered metadata and verified ZIP digests, including protocol and world
version. Keep current-generation success and error ownership unchanged.
Retain at most 128 MiB of decoded resource data and four content identities;
never retain resource managers, packs or open streams. Cache copies own their
arrays, and each resource generation receives independent bytes.

The 96 MiB encoded cache limit does not change source admission. Larger
source sets still reach the bounded codec without entering the cache.
Failed decodes remain retryable after the resource generation changes.

Regressions cover decode reuse, independent arrays across generations, changed
content, entry/byte eviction, uncacheable large source sets and failed decoding.

Preserve complete source maps and selected subpack metadata when caching
accepted native archives. Clone both views with shared-byte identity and
charge all retained source-only bytes to the same bounded cache budget.
The cache key covers ordered pack metadata and the digest of each nested
ZIP. Verify same-pack ZIP bytes before cache lookup, so changed or missing
unknown source files cannot be hidden by a decoded cache hit.

